// float-demo — the float package: figures and tables that float to a page edge or that text wraps around.
//
// \use{float} layers a document vocabulary over the engine's float primitives. \wrapfigure{side}{width}{body}
// anchors a figure against a margin so the running text wraps beside it; \figure{body} detaches a figure to the top
// of its page; and a \caption inside either one numbers it — figures and tables each on their own sequence — and
// files it into the list of figures. The hard parts (narrowing each line's measure, lifting a float to a page edge)
// live in the engine; the package is only the naming and numbering on top.

\use{document}
\use{float}

\title {Figures and Tables}
\author {the float package}
\maketitle

\section{Wrapping text around a figure}

\wrapfigure{r}{1.7in}{
  \picture width:1.7in height:1.2in {
    \fill{lightblue}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\rect{1 1 121 85}
  }
  \vskip 4pt
  \caption{A right-anchored figure.}
}
A wrapped figure stays in the running text: it anchors against the margin at the point it is written, and the
following lines flow around it in a narrowed measure. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed
do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation
ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Once the lines clear the bottom of the figure they return
to the full measure and run flush to the margin again.

\wrapfigure{l}{1.7in}{
  \picture width:1.7in height:1.2in {
    \fill{lightgreen}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\rect{1 1 121 85}
  }
  \vskip 4pt
  \caption{A left-anchored figure.}
}
The next figure anchors on the left, and its caption reads "Figure 2" — the count carries across figures wherever
they sit, wrapped or floated. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu
fugiat nulla pariatur. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit
anim id est laborum. Sed ut perspiciatis unde omnis iste natus error sit voluptatem accusantium doloremque.

\section{Wrapping around a shape}

An optional [shape] before the side — ellipse, triangleup, triangledown or diamond — makes the running text follow
the figure's outline rather than its bounding box. Draw a figure whose drawn shape matches the name; keep captions
out of a shaped figure, since the silhouette tapers away from the box's corners.

\wrapfigure[ellipse]{r}{1.5in}{
  \picture width:1.5in height:1.5in {
    \fill{lightblue}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\circle{54 54 54}
  }
}
A disc anchored on the right pushes the text away by its full width only across the middle; near the top and the foot
of the circle the silhouette curves back toward the margin and the text follows it in, taking a rounded bite out of
the column's edge. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut
labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip.

\wrapfigure[triangledown]{l}{1.5in}{
  \picture width:1.5in height:1.5in {
    \fill{lightgreen}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\polygon{0 0 0 108 108 108}
  }
}
A wedge on the left has its vertical leg on the margin and its hypotenuse sloping down to a point; the text to its
right follows that slope, each line stepping closer to the margin as the figure narrows beneath it. Excepteur sint
occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum, sed ut perspiciatis
unde omnis iste natus error sit voluptatem accusantium doloremque laudantium totam rem aperiam eaque ipsa quae.

\section{A floated figure and a table}

\figure{
  \centerline{
    \picture width:3in height:1in {
      \fill{lightyellow}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\rect{1 1 215 71}
    }
  }
  \vskip 4pt
  \centerline{\caption{A figure floated to the top of the page.}}
}
This figure was written here in the source but detaches to the top of the page, the way a LaTeX figure environment
floats. Its caption continues the figure sequence, so it is "Figure 3". Tables count on a separate sequence: the
caption below numbers as "Table 1", not "Figure 4".

\table{
  \centerline{
    \picture width:2in height:0.7in {
      \fill{lightgray}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\rect{1 1 143 49}
    }
  }
  \vskip 4pt
  \centerline{\caption{The same machinery numbers tables independently.}}
}
Both kinds of caption record themselves for a contents list, so the document package can print a list of figures,
as below.

\section{List of figures}

\listoffigs
